      <layerParams>
        <name>HiRISE Frost Prob. Ls 000-010 (Seasonal frost)</name>
        <category>Surface Properties</category>
        <subcategory>Frost Probability</subcategory>
        <topic>HIRISE</topic>
        <advancedOnly>false</advancedOnly>
        <type>map</type>
        <options>
          <string>hirise_frost_probability_ls_0-10_v2</string>
          <string>hirise_frost_probability_ls_0-10_v2</string>
        </options>
        <citation>Diniega, S., Wronkiewicz, M., Doran, G., Lu, S., Rebbapragada, U., Widmer, J. (2024, July 22-25) Integrating Thermal, Visible, and Spectral Observations to Create a Global Map of Martian Frost [3089]. Tenth International Conference on Mars, Pasadena, CA, United States. https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/tenthmars2024/pdf/3089.pdf Diniega et al., submitted, Holistic Mapping of the Present-day Martian Seasonal CO2 Frost: Part 1, Frost Detection within Global Visible, Thermal, and Spectral Datasets, Planetary Science Journal. Diniega et al., in prep, Holistic Mapping of the Present-day Martian Seasonal CO2 Frost: Part 2, Integration of Visible, Thermal, and Spectral-based Frost Detections, Planetary Science Journal. 
</citation>
        <description>This map includes an estimate of seasonal frost likelihood (expressed as a probability from 0-1 that frost is present within the spatiotemporal pixels) for regions spanning the edge of the seasonal frostcaps and into the mid-latitudes between Ls range [0-10]. It relies on daytime (~3pm) data acquired by the specified instrument and represents the average frost likelihood across Mars years 29-35. Note that CO2 and/or water frost may be present in a frost detection. Source data were obtained from the PDS. It is one of several frost likelihood maps generated based on Thermal (MCS/THEMIS), Spectral (CRISM), and Visible (HiRISE, CTX) data.  Date of ASU ingest: 11/12/2024
</description>
